00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
hiya my name is elizabeth i'm a medical  student and an artist living in london  and in this video i wanted to go through  the top 10 things i wish i'd known  before starting  medical school now these are only my own  personal opinion  so please bear with me but if you're  planning on going to medical school  hopefully these will serve useful to you  and if you're already a medical student  or a doctor and you've felt  the same way please let me know i love  finding out that i'm not the only person  in the world who  experiences things a certain way and yes  those are my two goals here  hopefully i can get some of it  accomplished let's get straight into it  the first thing i wish i'd known is that  medical school is not as hard as they  say  now this is a very controversial one  because i think that stereotype of the  overworked underslept struggling to  understand  medical student is so so prevalent and  strong  but i did not find that this was  necessarily the case for me i wish i'd  known  that the things that we need to  understand in medicine are actually  not that difficult the last time i  struggled to understand something was  probably in high school with further  maths and advanced physics but it was  definitely never in  university the difficulty only lies in  the workload but even that  is not as bad as it seems or as  exaggerated as  people make it out to be i wish i could  have told myself that you'll be  absolutely fine it is not that  terrifying or difficult  and yeah i think this would have calmed  me down a lot  the next thing i wish i'd known is how  much free time i would have in  medical school i thought that i was  signing off my life for five to six  years  and i would be unable to do anything  else this was absolutely  not the case i've been working full-time  while i've been in medical school  alongside it i've also been involved in  so many different projects when i  started first year  i felt a bit guilty with all of my free  time  and i wish i would have known beforehand  that actually  you the busiest time of your life is  probably not going to be in medical  school the next thing i wish i'd  appreciated is  how long medical school is now  when it comes to this a lot of people  have told me oh i could never study  something that lasts five years or six  years and i thought well you know that's  not a big deal  and it definitely isn't and it's not the  length of medical school  in itself that i want to talk about but  it's rather  what doing a single thing for five years  will do to  you so the way that i would like to  describe this is for example  if we think of ourselves today and we  think of ourselves five years ago  it's very very likely that those two  people are extremely  different and this has definitely been  the case for me  in my second year of medical school i  found that all the reasons  for which i've wanted to do medicine  have disappeared  or changed or i fully disagree with them  so i think that if you do anything over  five years or even if you do nothing  over five years  you will definitely change a lot and you  might find  that the person that you become does not  feel the same way about your university  or about your workload or about what  they want to do for the rest of their  lives  so this was something that i really  hadn't considered i started medicine  thinking i would always have the same  view on life and the same desires and  needs and that definitely changed so  i think having a bit more of an open  mind when i started  is something i wish i'd done the next  thing which ties onto this  is that i wish i'd believe people when  they told me that it's very unlikely  that i'm going to want to do the  specialty  that i got into medicine wanting i  wanted to be a surgeon for the longest  time and when i started medicine a lot  of people would tell me oh you just wait  you're going to change your mind  and to me that was very offensive  because i thought that that meant  them saying that i'm not smart enough to  be a surgeon i'm not hardworking enough  to be a surgeon or just because i  am a female that i would want to have  children and  kind of not want to work out of hours or  have a career that takes so much time  out of my life  and for all of these reasons i stuck  very firmly to it and got offended  every time someone would tell me that  now i wish i could have seen  beyond that emotional response into  realizing  that things change and the things that  you appreciate or like about medicine  are also likely to change  and therefore your specialty might also  not be the same  now having said that i know that there  are people who had you know  their mind set to be a pediatrician at  the age of three and still are very very  happily during that career at the age of  70  but for the most of us we change a lot  and the things that we like to change a  lot and we might grow particularly  attached to a specialty just because we  have these amazing consultants that make  it seem as though it is the best thing  in the world  or we might change our mind again and  again and all of these things are  absolutely normal i think that being not  too  attached to one specialty when you start  to medical school is a good  idea because then if you do change your  mind you won't go through an identity  crisis like i did  thinking that oh my god what do i do now  when i started questioning whether  surgery was  right for me by the way it still remains  one of my favorite medical specialties  it's just that i'm not sure that that is  the career that i want for the rest of  my life  the next thing i wish i'd known and i  think that this is the most important to  me  is that the things that people tell you  are going to be difficult in medicine  are not actually the most difficult  thing in medicine  so i thought that seeing a dead body or  dissecting a dead body or being in  surgery  or having to stress over exams or having  such a huge workload was going to make  medicine very very challenging for me  the reality was very very different i  absolutely  loved dissection i got used to the smell  of quarterization  after my first surgery and i've never  stressed the slightest over  exams that's not to say that medicine  has been easy it was just things that i  did not expect to encounter  things that stayed with me the most and  got the most under my skin  and kept me up for weeks where certain  encounters with patients were in  i felt as though we weren't really  making a difference seeing patients who  are being abused for example but who  refuse to report that abuse  while you treat their asthma is  something that has kept me up or cases  in psychiatry where you do feel a huge  sense of helplessness  and hopelessness are the things that i  find the most challenging about medicine  and i was not prepared for this at all  the next thing i wish i'd known is how  expensive medical school can be  and i'm definitely biased here because i  live in london  but it's insane rent on its own  paying for food and the thing about  london is that hospitals are so  scattered  so for example in the same day i need to  go to one part of london for my lectures  another part of london for training  another part of london to  process data for the study another part  of london to work  and then go home which is obviously not  close to any of these  every day takes a lot of money and time  and on top of that you do need to pay  for all of the extra training or  all the extra diplomas you want to do  for example any sort of extra thing you  want to do  i had a poster up at a conference last  year going to that conference and  putting it up on its own  cost about 400 pounds so i did  definitely not appreciate how even  wanting to do  extra things that would be good for  medical school would cost me  so much money i'm working on the side at  minimum wage is a very difficult way to  make up for this  so i definitely did not appreciate the  financial difficulties  that i would be facing at times in  medical school the next thing i wish i'd  known  is that you still need to do things that  you don't  want to do a lot of you may be a lot  more mature than i was in high school  but i remember passionately feeling in  plant biology which i hated that i  cannot wait to go to university to study  only the thing that i care about which  is the human body and be done  with all these topics that i could not  care about less  this is not necessarily the case there  will always be things that you do not  find as interesting  in my case that is microbiology for  example but also  things like studies i absolutely hate  sitting in front of a computer and  processing numbers for hours and hours  and weeks and weeks  i find it incredibly boring but this is  something that i have to do  as a medical student and i will have to  do as a doctor in the future too so  so i think being a bit calmer and not as  dramatic and  lowering my expectations for now i only  get to do what i want once i go into  medical school  would have been a bit more realistic in  my case the next thing  that i wasn't ready for was how people  were going to treat me once i started  saying i'm  a medical student if any of you have  experienced this kind of  look or the embodiment of the stereotype  of oh  you must be so serious and you must be  so hard working  and oh you must have no free time you  must be so sleep deprived  and i mean the stereotypes can go both  ways i felt that i was not ready to be  thrown in  to this medical stereotype so soon and  the next thing i wish i'd known  is that medical school although it's  five years  is not actually like any other  university degree  if you're thinking you're going to get  that university experience  it's probably not going to happen in  medicine and i wish i'd known this  i started out so slow paced thinking oh  i've got five to six years ahead of me  i don't need to engage as much in  university activities but  at king's for example we only have one  year of pre-clinical medicine  and after that you're almost never in  university at all  your projects are for yourself to do on  your own your learning is  is almost only with practice in hospital  and you're not feeling that university  experience  so if you think that oh you're going to  get six years of what others get for  three years  that may not necessarily be the case if  i've known this i would have definitely  appreciated my first year  a bit more and the last thing i wish i'd  known  is something that would have given me so  much hope and that is just how  incredible people are in medicine so  many medical professionals and medical  students and people that i get to spend  time with are absolutely mind-blowingly  inspiring they're passionate about what  i'm passionate in but a lot of them are  hundreds of times better than i am at it  and i find this  so so so inspiring there's so many  opportunities to learn  and to feel good when you see people  excelling at what they do  and definitely this is something that i  am endlessly grateful for  so yes those were 10 of the many many  realizations that i've had in medical  school  hopefully if you're planning to be a  medical student any one of these will  help you  or apologies if they do shatter any of  your expectations  so yeah as always if you made it so far  thank you so so much for spending this  time with me i hope you have  a wonderful rest of your day be kind  yourself and others and don't believe  everything you think  thanks bye

